    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d922">

                <pb facs="0889" n="575" sameAs="frus:frus1914Supp/pg575"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72111/643</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The German Ambassador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Bernstorff</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">German Embassy</hi>, <lb/>
                    <placeName>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                        calendar="gregorian" when="1914-11-11">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">November 11, 1914</hi>.</date>
                    <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received November 12</hi>.] </dateline>
                <p rendition="#center">[Translation]</p>
                <p rendition="#flush">J. No. A 2116]</p>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Mr. Secretary of State</hi>: I have the honor to
                    draw your excellency’s attention to the announcement made in No. 260 of the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Daily Consular and Trade Reports</hi>, published by the
                    Department of Commerce, under No. 14333 of “Foreign Trade Opportunities,” page
                    607, of the opportunity to deliver war material to the English
                        Government.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d922-f1"/>
                </p>
                <p>Since the offices of the Bureau of Foreign and Domestic Trade are, as I have come
                    to learn, at the disposal of the agents of foreign governments to help the
                    transaction of business between them and American manufacturers, I beg your
                    excellency kindly to let me know whether their activities as go-betweens also
                    include delivery of war material.</p>
                <p>Accept [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">J. Bernstorff</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d922-f1" n="1" xml:id="d922-f1-content">
                <p>For the substance of the item referred to, see the Secretary’s answer of January
                    15, 1915, <hi rendition="#italic">post</hi>, <ref target="#pg579">p.
                    579</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
